## Getting Started

### 1. Installation

Clone or locate the repository and install requirements:

```bash
git clone <repo-url>
cd VisualSeek
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

### 2. Inspect Dataset

Verify that the Stanford Dogs dataset is properly discovered:

```bash
python scripts/inspect_dataset.py
```

### 3. Generate Embeddings

Generate L2-normalized 512-dimensional CLIP embeddings (default: 1,000 images in development mode):

```bash
python scripts/generate_embeddings.py
```

To run on the full 20,580 images:
```bash
python scripts/generate_embeddings.py --limit all
```

### 4. Build FAISS Index

Build the vector similarity index:

```bash
python scripts/build_index.py
```

### 5. Precompute Dimensionality Reductions

Generate PCA and t-SNE coordinates for the Embedding Explorer:

```bash
python scripts/run_reduction.py
```

### 6. Run Evaluation Benchmarks

Evaluate Precision@K and compare against baseline histograms:

```bash
python scripts/evaluate.py
```

### 7. Launch the Application

Launch the Streamlit interface:

```bash
streamlit run app/main.py
```

---

## Evaluation & Baseline Comparison

Retrieval performance is empirically evaluated using leave-one-out testing on 50 sample queries across 500 Stanford Dogs images:

| Metric | Color Histogram Baseline | OpenAI CLIP ViT-B/32 | Improvement Factor |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| **Embedding Dimension** | 128 | 512 | 4.0x Capacity |
| **Precision@1** | 2.0% | **10.0%** | **5.0x** |
| **Precision@5** | 0.8% | **8.4%** | **10.5x** |
| **Precision@10** | 0.8% | **7.2%** | **9.0x** |
| **Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR)** | 0.0356 | **0.2116** | **5.9x** |
| **FAISS Query Latency** | 0.03 ms | 0.06 ms | Sub-millisecond |

> **Methodology Note**: Same-breed classification is an objective proxy signal. In open-world semantic retrieval, cross-breed visual and semantic similarities (e.g., small white coats, erect pointed ears, posture) are inherently captured by joint vision-language projections.

---

## Known Limitations & Design Trade-offs

1. **Semantic vs. Breed Granularity**: CLIP prioritizes visual semantic features (color, fur texture, pose, context). Two distinct breeds with white coats (e.g. West Highland Terrier and Maltese) will have high cosine similarity (~0.85).
2. **Background Influence**: Complex outdoor backgrounds (green grass, snow, sand) contribute slightly to the latent vector alongside the dog subject.
3. **Hardware & Sizing**: The application defaults to a 1,000-image development sample (`limit: 1000`) for sub-second retrieval and instant startup on CPU. Toggling `limit: null` indexes all 20,580 images.

